    "knowledge_point": "Differentiating an Exponential Function with a Nonlinear Exponent",
    "problem": "Given that $y={e}^{{x}^{2}-2x},$ find $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$",
    "worked_solution": "First, we express $y$ as the composite function\n\n$$\ny={e}^{u},\\,u={x}^{2}-2x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}={e}^{u},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x-2.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}u}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & ={e}^{u}\\cdot (2x-2) \\\\  & =(2x-2){e}^{u} \\\\  & =(2x-2){e}^{{x}^{2}-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
